I believe that someone else has a very in depth explanation, so I will
keep mine short and different. Shakespearean English is Modern English
(1500s to now (roughly)),
Old English (600 -1100) would be Beowulf.
Middle English (1100 - 1500ish) is Chaucer.

So instead of looking up Old English, keywords would be Shakespearean,
King James, or Archaic.

Shorter answer is that it is a little bit more like German than
Contemporary English. Most of the
wierd stuff is second person
(extremely formal) Nobody talks that way anymore except for gods and
such... It is like Thou is the "Siezen" form of You (the
"duzen" form). So just substitute "Y" for "Th" and see if it sounds better. (or substitute M
for Th, and know that it switches to first person).

For Verbs, just de-conjugate using German methods on the English Verb and
expect the vowels to be screwed up.


